ccess denials logged (audit logging through server logs) — bundled HLR-8.\n- Per-request workspace resolution overhead measured; short-TTL caching implemented if overhead exceeds the threshold agreed with PM/Engineering (OQ1 handling).\n- Non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled in artifacts-only mode; tracking/model registry stores uninitialized.\n- Rollback/compat: no existing flags change meaning (Rule 23 — replaces existing capability? It extends, doesn't replace. Rule 23: \"Strategy replaces existing capability → add rollback/feature-flag AC\". Here behavior only activates when both flags set — the flag combination IS the feature flag. AC: omitting either flag preserves current behavior — already AC3. Good, note that.)\n\nE004 (Implementation, plugin):\n- Workspace resolution in the plugin functions in artifacts-only context without tracking store initialization (verify; refactor if coupled — per investigation outcome).\n- If refactoring needed: workspace resolution factored into an independent initialization path within the plugin, preserving existing behavior for the full tracking server (no regression to tracking-server workspace isolation).\n- Existing auth plugin behavior for tracking server unchanged (regression).\n- kube-rbac-proxy / --app-name kubernetes-auth setup unchanged.\n\nE005 (Implementation, operator):\n- AC5: MLflow CR with split-server artifact serving enabled → operator creates dedicated artifacts-only Deployment, Service, HTTPRoute separate from main tracking Deployment (distinct Deployments with correct flag combinations).\n- AC6: artifacts-only Deployment container command runs `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes:// --app-name kubernetes-auth --serve-artifacts` (inspect rendered Deployment spec).\n- Main tracking deployment configured to route artifact traffic per the routing design.\n- Operator validation rejects configurations where the artifacts-only instance lacks workspace flags (risk mitigation).