required chart changes identified).\n- The decision is recorded with alternatives considered and reviewed by the MLflow Team.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-6 (P1): determines how the main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated instance.\n- HLR-5 (P1): informs the HTTPRoute design for the dedicated artifacts-only instance.\n\nE003 body:\n\n## Description\nMakes the MLflow tracking server's `--artifacts-only` mode composable with `--enable-workspaces` (and `--workspace-store-uri`), so the artifacts-only server validates artifact requests against workspace/tenant boundaries: workspace-aware, fail-closed artifact serving with no new server flag.\n\n## Scope\n- Combine `--artifacts-only` with `--enable-workspaces`: when both set, require explicit `--workspace-store-uri`, initialize only the workspace provider needed to validate artifact requests; tracking and model registry stores stay uninitialized; non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled. Fail to start with a clear error if `--enable-workspaces` is set without `--workspace-store-uri`.\n- Workspace validation in artifact request handling: resolve artifact path to parent experiment, look up experiment's workspace assignment via the workspace store, delegate authorization to existing auth plugin infrastructure; denials return HTTP 403 with insufficient-workspace-permissions error.\n- Fail-closed behavior: if workspace store unreachable while workspace-aware mode active, deny requests (HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation unavailable) rather than serve without validation.\n- Backwards compatibility: `--artifacts-only` without `--enable-workspaces` retains current behavior; single-tenant/unset-flag deployments unaffected; no existing CLI arguments or config keys change meaning.\n- Audit logging of workspace access denials through server logs (P2).\n- Measure per-request workspace resolution overhead; implement short-TTL caching of workspace membership decisions if overhead exceeds the threshold agreed with PM/Engineering (consistent with caching patterns already used in the tracking server's auth path).\n- Initial implementation scoped to the S3 artifact store (standard RHOAI configuration) with documented limitations for non-standard artifact roots.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- (AC1) artifacts-only instance started with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes://`; user member of workspace A requests artifact of experiment in workspace A → HTTP 200 with correct artifact content.\n- (AC2) user member of workspace A but not B requests artifact of experiment in workspace B → HTTP 403 with error indicating insufficient workspace permissions.\n- (AC3) `--artifacts-only` without `--enable-workspaces`: authenticated user requests artifact → served without workspace validation (HTTP 200 regardless of workspace membership) — current behavior preserved.\n- (AC4) workspace store (Kubernetes API) unreachable while workspace-aware serving enabled → request denied with HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation unavailable.\n- Server started with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces` but no `--workspace-store-uri` fails to start with a clear error.\n- Requests with invalid or missing workspace context are denied, not served.\n- Workspace access denials are logged through server logs.\n- Non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled and tracking/model registry stores remain uninitialized in combined mode.\n- Per-request workspace resolution overhead is measured and reported; short-TTL caching implemented if the agreed threshold is exceeded.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-1 (P0): flag combination for workspace-validated artifact serving.\n- HLR-2 (P0): workspace membership validation with HTTP 403 denials.\n- HLR-3 (P0): fail-closed behavior on unreachable workspace store.\n- HLR-4 (P0): single-tenant / no-workspace-flag behavior unchanged.\n- HLR-8 (P2, bundled): audit logging of workspace access denials through server logs — bundled because denials are emitted from the same workspace-validation denial path in the artifact request handler this epic builds; a separate epic would instrument the same call sites twice, and the logging has no independent user surface.\n\n## Depends on (plain terms)\nHmm — no cross-references. I can mention in Description or a \"Dependencies\" section in plain terms: \"Requires the workspace resolution capability in the auth plugin to function without tracking store initialization, and the feasibility findings on workspace resolution in the artifacts-only context.\" I'll add a short \"Dependencies and Assumptions\" section in plain terms. Actually the minimum sections are the four; extra allowed. I'll add brief plain-language dependency notes. Careful not to name sibling epic IDs.\n\nE004 body:\n\n## Description\nMakes the mlflow-kubernetes-plugins auth plugin's workspace resolution function correctly in the artifacts-only server context, where tracking and model registry stores are not initialized — verifying the current initialization path and refactoring it if workspace resolution is coupled to full tracking server initialization.\n\n## Scope\n- Verify the plugin's workspace resolution (experiment-to-workspace mapping via the workspace store, registered through the `mlflow.workspace_provider` entry point group and loaded by WorkspaceStoreRegistry) in a server context without tracking/model registry stores.\n- If coupled to full server initialization: factor workspace resolution into an independent initialization path within the plugin, per the feasibility findings.\n- Preserve existing plugin behavior for the full tracking server (no regression to workspace isolation for non-artifact operations).\n- kube-rbac-proxy sidecar and `--app-name kubernetes-auth` authentication setup unchanged — reuse existing auth plugin infrastructure; no prescription of Kubernetes API mechanics (per Staff Engineer input, SSAR detail is an implementation detail left to the plugin team).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Plugin workspace resolution returns correct experiment-to-workspace mapping in an artifacts-only server context with tracking and model registry stores uninitialized.\n- If refactoring was required: workspace resolution initializes via an independent path, and existing tracking-server workspace isolation behavior is unchanged (regression verified).\n- Existing authentication behavior (kube-rbac-proxy sidecar, `--app-name kubernetes-auth`) is unchanged.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-7 (P1): auth plugin workspace resolution functions correctly in the artifacts-only context without tracking store initialization.\n- Enables HLR-2/HLR-3 (P0) transitively: the server's workspace validation and fail-closed artifact serving build on this capability (priority raised to P0 by inheritance).\n\nE005 body:\n\n## Description\nExtends the MLflow operator to deploy a dedicated artifacts-only MLflow instance as a separate Deployment with its own Service and HTTPRoute (split-server topology), and configures the main tracking deployment to route artifact traffic to that instance — per the routing design.\n\n## Scope\n- New Helm chart templates for the artifacts-only Deployment, Service, and HTTPRoute, rendered through the operator's existing Helm-in-operator / Server-Side Apply mechanism.\n- Configure the main tracking deployment to route artifact traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance per the routing design.\n- Propagate workspace configuration (`--enable-workspaces`, `--workspace-store-uri`, `--app-name kubernetes-auth`) to the artifacts-only instance using the same configuration mechanisms as the main tracking deployment.\n- Gateway API HTTPRoute resources created conditionally based on runtime CRD discovery (existing pattern for optional dependencies).\n- Operator validation rejecting configurations where the artifacts-only instance lacks workspace flags (misconfiguration could let artifact requests bypass workspace validation).\n- Backwards compatibility: MLflow CRs without split-server artifact serving reconcile unchanged (single-deployment behavior preserved).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Given an MLflow CR with split-server artifact serving enabled, reconciliation creates a dedicated artifacts-only Deployment, Service, and HTTPRoute separate from the main tracking Deployment — measured by presence of distinct Deployment resources with the correct flag combinations.\n- The artifacts-only Deployment's container command runs `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes:// --app-name kubernetes-auth --serve-artifacts` — measured by inspecting the rendered Deployment spec.\n- The main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated artifacts-only instance per the adopted routing design.\n- The operator rejects CR configurations that enable the artifacts-only instance without workspace flags.\n- MLflow CRs without split-server configuration reconcile to the current single-deployment topology (no change).\n- End-to-end verification: workspace-aware artifact serving works through the operator-deployed split topology (verification runs against the workspace-aware artifacts-only server build; this is an acceptance gate on this epic's deliverable, not a dependency on the server-side work).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-5 (P1): dedicated artifacts-only instance as separate Deployment/Service/HTTPRoute.\n- HLR-6 (P1): main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated instance.\n\nSignal rationale files. Let me draft each.\n\nE001-ai-signals.md (investigation signals):\n| Signal | Value | Rationale |\n| question_specificity | +1 | Three concrete, enumerable questions with pass/fail criteria: (1) does plugin/workspace-store initialization depend on tracking stores (yes/no), (2) do artifact request paths carry resolvable experiment/run IDs under the S3 layout, (3) can path-based resolution be bypassed. |\n| source_accessibility | +1 | Answers are determined by readable source: the MLflow midstream repo (red-hat-data-services/mlflow), the mlflow-kubernetes-plugins source, and the mlflow.md architecture context documenting the workspace provider entry point and kubernetes:// workspace store. |\n| local_runnability | 0 | The MLflow server and plugin are pip-installable, but the kubernetes:// workspace store is backed by the Kubernetes API — exercising workspace resolution end-to-end requires a cluster (e.g. a local dev cluster), not just a downloaded library. |\n| cluster_hardware_dependence | -1 | The gating questions are answerable from source, but peripheral confirmation of workspace-store behavior without tracking stores realistically needs a live (local dev) Kubernetes API. |\n| human_judgment_required | 0 | All questions are technical and owned by the MLflow Team; no stakeholder/product or external-party decision gates the outcome. |\n\nE002-ai-signals.md:\n| question_specificity | +1 | Concrete question with a defined completion criterion: a routing design for artifact traffic from the main tracking deployment to the dedicated instance, including an explicit determination of whether the tracking server needs a configuration change. |\n| source_accessibility | +1 | Determined by readable artifacts: MLflow server source (artifact serving/routing mechanisms), operator source, and mlflow-operator.md documenting the Helm-in-operator and conditional HTTPRoute patterns. |\n| local_runnability | 0 | The routing design is desk analysis, but validating candidate HTTPRoute/gateway behavior requires a deployed cluster topology with Gateway API. |\n| cluster_hardware_dependence | -1 | Confirming the chosen routing design (HTTPRoute path routing vs server delegation) against Gateway API requires a live cluster; the design decision itself does not. |\n| human_judgment_required | 0 | The routing approach is a technical design decision owned by the MLflow Team; no external stakeholder or governance approval gates it (team review of the design is