{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-992c37dc789a40d9","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\n3\tepic_count: 5\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8009: Workspace-aware artifact serving for multi-tenant MLflow deployments\n10\t\n11\tStrategy: RHAISTRAT-8009 (Critical, In Progress). Source RFE: RHAIRFE-7127.\n12\tAll work is owned by the MLflow Team across three strategy components (MLflow,\n13\tMLflow Operator, mlflow-kubernetes-plugins).\n14\t\n15\t## Staff Engineer Input Overrides\n16\t\n17\tThe strategy's Staff Engineer / SME Input is non-empty and takes precedence. The\n18\tTechnical Approach in the strategy already reflects all three corrections; the\n19\tdecomposition follows them and logs them for traceability:\n20\t\n21\t1. **No new flag** — the decomposition contains no `--artifacts-only-workspace-aware`\n22\t   flag and no `spec.artifactsOnly.workspaceAware` CR field anywhere. The server-side\n23\t   epic's scope is combining the existing `--artifacts-only` and `--enable-workspaces`\n24\t   flags; the operator epic propagates existing flags only.\n25\t2. **Operator work is a split deployment, not a config toggle** — the operator epic\n26\t   deploys a dedicated artifacts-only instance as a separate Deployment with its own\n27\t   Service and HTTPRoute and configures artifact traffic routing, not a single CR toggle.\n28\t3. **SSAR detail is over-specified** — the plugin epic's scope states that workspace\n29\t   authorization reuses the existing auth plugin infrastructure without dictating\n30\t   Kubernetes API mechanics (no SelfSubjectAccessReview prescriptions).\n31\t\n32\tNo conflicts between Staff Engineer Input and the architecture context were detected.\n33\t\n34\t## Component Mappings\n35\t\n36\t`.context/rhai-components.txt` contains exactly one MLflow-related canonical\n37\tcomponent: `MLflow`. All three strategy components therefore map to it (mapping\n38\tlogged per Step 2; `submit.py` silently strips non-canonical names):\n39\t\n40\t| Strategy component | Canonical component | Note |\n41\t|----|----|----|\n42\t| MLflow | MLflow | direct |\n43\t| MLflow Operator | MLflow | no separate canonical component exists; operator work is the single most-relevant canonical name |\n44\t| mlflow-kubernetes-plugins | MLflow | no separate canonical component exists; plugin work is the single most-relevant canonical name |\n45\t\n46\t`mlflow-kubernetes-plugins` has no dedicated architecture-context file, but it is\n47\tnot a new component to provision: it already ships inside the platform as the\n48\texternal `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins` PyPI package (v1.5.0) consumed by the MLflow\n49\timage (mlflow.md, Dockerfile.konflux / requirements/konflux-pypi.in). No\n50\trepo-onboarding / konflux-onboarding / license-validation chain is generated; the\n51\tplugin's changes are general implementation work in its existing source repository.\n52\tAll strategy dependencies are internal and already exist, so no provisioning or\n53\tonboarding epics are generated for any component.\n54\t\n55\t## Epic List\n56\t\n57\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n58\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n59\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E001 | Validate workspace resolution in the artifacts-only server context | Investigation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n60\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E002 | Design artifact traffic routing for the split-server topology | Investigation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n61\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E003 | Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n62\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E004 | Artifacts-only workspace resolution in the mlflow-kubernetes-plugins auth plugin | Implementation | MLflow Team | P0 |\n63\t| RHAISTRAT-8009-E005 | Split-server artifacts-only deployment topology in the MLflow operator | Implementation | MLflow Team | P1 |\n64\t\n65\t## Dependency DAG\n66\t\n67\t```mermaid\n68\tgraph TD\n69\t    E001[\"E001 Investigation: workspace resolution feasibility\"] --> E004[\"E004 Plugin: artifacts-only workspace resolution\"]\n70\t    E001 --> E003[\"E003 Server: workspace-aware artifacts-only serving\"]\n71\t    E004 --> E003\n72\t    E002[\"E002 Investigation: artifact traffic routing design\"] --> E005[\"E005 Operator: split-server topology\"]\n73\t```\n74\t\n75\tCritical path: E001 → E004 → E003 (length 3).\n76\t\n77\t## DAG Justification\n78\t\n79\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n80\t|------|------|-----------|\n81\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates affected Implementations) | The feasibility investigation determines whether the plugin's workspace resolution is coupled to tracking-store initialization, i.e. whether E004 is verification-only or a refactor — a true gate with `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite`. |\n82\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 | The investigation confirms (or redirects) the server's path-based workspace validation approach (artifact path → experiment → workspace resolvability, bypass analysis) — true gate, `rewrite`. |\n83\t| E004 → E003 | Rule 12 (framework/library → consumer serial) | The server's artifact request handling builds against the plugin's workspace resolution API (workspace provider loaded via `WorkspaceStoreRegistry`); the consumer waits for the library capability. |\n84\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 3 | The routing design investigation determines the operator epic's approach (HTTPRoute design, whether the tracking deployment needs a delegation configuration change) — true gate, `rewrite`. |\n85\t| No edge E002 → E003 | Rule 4 / Step 6.5 | The routing question's outcome affects how the *tracking deployment is configured* (operator-owned Helm values), not the server-side flag-composition and validation code. If the outcome does require a server-side code change, that scope lands in E003 as an addition — recorded as a health warning, not a DAG edge that would serialize independent server work. |\n86\t| No edge E005 ↔ E003/E004 | Rules 13/14 (config references are AC gates, not edges) | The operator propagates configuration (flag strings, URIs) to its Deployments; it does not build against server or plugin code. The end-to-end coupling (\"workspace-aware serving works through the operator-deployed topology\") is an acceptance criterion on E005, never a DAG dependency — the chart/reconciler work proceeds in parallel with the server work. |\n87\t| No fail-mode / security Investigation epics (Rule 19) | Rule 4 reclassification | The strategy is security-relevant (tenant isolation), but the fail-mode behavior is already fully specified and mandated (fail-closed, HTTP 503, NFR Security section, AC 4) — there is no unknown to investigate. The security question that IS unresolved (whether path-based experiment resolution can be bypassed with crafted paths) is folded into E001's scope as an explicit deliverable rather than a separate epic, because it is resolved by the same source analysis that determines path resolvability. |\n88\t\n89\t## Investigation Outcomes (bounded, not branched)\n90\t\n91\tE001 has bounded outcomes (≤3) that change downstream **scope**, not the epic set:\n92\t(A) plugin decoupled + paths resolvable → E004 reduces to verification; (B) plugin\n93\tcoupled to full server initialization → E004 refactors the initialization path;\n94\t(C) artifact paths not resolvable to workspaces → E003's validation approach\n95\trewrites. Because the same epics exist in every outcome (only scope/approach\n96\tvaries), the outcomes are expressed via `gate_failure_impact` on the gated epics\n97\trather than conditional branch files. E002's outcome may additionally add\n98\ttracking-deployment delegation configuration to E005's scope.\n99\t\n100\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n101\t\n102\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n103\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n104\t| HLR-1 `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` + `--workspace-store-uri` | P0 | E003 | |\n105\t| HLR-2 Artifact requests validated against workspace membership; non-members denied HTTP 403 | P0 | E003 | enabled by E004's plugin capability (library → consumer edge) |\n106\t| HLR-3 Fail-closed: workspace store unreachable → requests denied | P0 | E003 | |\n107\t| HLR-4 Single-tenant / no-workspace-flag deployments retain current behavior | P0 | E003 | |\n108\t| HLR-5 Operator deploys dedicated artifacts-only Deployment + Service + HTTPRoute | P1 | E005 | |\n109\t| HLR-6 Main tracking deployment routes artifact traffic to the dedicated instance | P1 | E005 | approach determined by E002 |\n110\t| HLR-7 Auth plugin workspace resolution works without tracking store initialization | P1 | E004 | raised to P0 by priority inheritance (blocks P0 epic E003) |\n111\t| HLR-8 Audit logging for workspace access denials through server logs | P2 | E003 (bundled) — denial logging is emitted from the same workspace-validation denial path in the artifact request handler that HLR-2/HLR-3 build; a separate epic would instrument the same call sites twice, and the log lines have no separate user surface, so it is not independently schedulable | |\n112\t\n113\tAll 8 HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements. No `docs-authoring` epic:\n114\tthe strategy contains no substantive content-authoring scope and `Documentation\n115\tSupport` is \"Pending human review\", not \"Yes\" — incidental doc touch-ups fall out\n116\tof the code epics as scope/ACs.\n117\t\n118\t## Health Warnings\n119\t\n120\t1. **Priority label vs. inheritance (plugin work)** — the strategy marks the auth\n121\t   plugin resolution HLR as P1, but the plugin capability is a prerequisite of the\n122\t   P0 server epic (HLR-2/HLR-3 build on it), so E004 is P0 by the inheritance rule.\n123\t   Planners should treat the plugin work as P0 despite its P1 label in the HLR list.\n124\t2. **Epic size above the ~2-week guideline (E005)** — E005 is 2-3 sprints by the\n125\t   strategy's effort arithmetic; kept whole because the artifacts-only\n126\t   Deployment/Service/HTTPRoute templates, the artifact traffic routing\n127\t   configuration, and the CR validation all render from the same Helm chart and\n128\t   reconciler surface, and a split would duplicate chart scaffolding and fragment\n129\t   one reconcile loop; re-split trigger: if the routing-design investigation lands\n130\t   a design requiring tracking-server-side delegation changes or the chart work\n131\t   exceeds the strategy's 3-sprint estimate for the operator scope, split artifact\n132\t   traffic routing into its own epic.\n133\t3. **Strategy-level process gates pending human review** — Architecture Review,\n134\t   ODH/RHOAI Build Onboarding, Licence Validation, and Documentation Support are\n135\t   all \"Pending human review\". They are not decomposed into epics because every\n136\t   component already builds and ships in the platform (Dockerfile.konflux and\n137\t   konflux workflows per mlflow.md / mlflow-operator.md); the pending reviews are\n138\t   reflected in the `human_process_gates` signal on the affected epics.\n139\t4. **Proposed acceptance criteria require PM/Engineering validation** — the\n140\t   strategy's AC section is explicitly marked \"requires PM/Engineering validation\";\n141\t   the ACs are carried into the epics as written and may be adjusted in review.\n142\t5. **No latency target (open question, PM/Engineering)** — handled as a\n143\t   measurement AC plus a conditional short-TTL caching trigger on E003 rather than\n144\t   an Investigation: the answer changes only whether the incremental caching\n145\t   optimization ships at launch, not which epics exist or their approach. If a\n146\t   strict target is set before implementation, E003's caching scope may grow.\n147\t6. **Plugin repository not covered by architecture context** — mlflow-kubernetes-\n148\t   plugins has no architecture-context file; its role is corroborated indirectly via\n149\t   mlflow.md, but its source repository location/access is unconfirmed\n150\t   (`repo_access=0` on E004). Confirm the repo before scheduling E004.\n151\t7. **Routing outcome may add server-side scope** — if E002 concludes the tracking\n152\t   server needs a code change (not just operator-owned configuration) to delegate\n153\t   artifact operations, that scope is added to E003 rather than being pre-serialized\n154\t   via a DAG edge.\n155\t\n156\t## Tiered Delivery\n157\t\n158\tNot applicable in the Step 5.5 sense: there is no external dependency with\n159\tuncertain timing — all four strategy dependencies (workspace store,\n160\tmlflow-kubernetes-plugins auth plugin, Gateway API, kube-rbac-proxy sidecar) are\n161\tinternal and already exist. The P0/P1 split in the epic list is intrinsic\n162\tpriority, and it does yield a natural phasing: the P0 server-side chain\n163\t(E001 → E004 → E003) delivers workspace-aware artifact serving for manually\n164\tconfigured deployments on its own; the P1 operator chain (E002 → E005) automates\n165\tthe split-server deployment topology on top of it.\n166\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bc40f63e2747ee2e","agent_id":"a2a87ed65c879dd0a","session_id":"acc56831-ee8f-4f4b-89df-cab5630bc453","uuid":"4b8753f6-4306-4bca-9371-fe0e24cf2fb8","timestamp":"2026-10-26T18:39:09.072Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-992c37dc789a40d9","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8009 decomposition"}
