{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8b0bbf8732fcb798","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8009-E003\n3\ttitle: Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8009\n5\tcomponent: MLflow\n6\tteam: MLflow Team\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies:\n10\t- RHAISTRAT-8009-E001\n11\t- RHAISTRAT-8009-E004\n12\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8009-E001\n13\tgate_failure_impact:\n14\t  action: rewrite\n15\t  fallback_approach: If the feasibility findings show artifact request paths cannot\n16\t    be resolved to workspace membership or that workspace validation cannot run without\n17\t    tracking stores, redesign the validation approach for workspace-aware artifact\n18\t    serving before implementing.\n19\tai_signals:\n20\t  change_specificity: 1\n21\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n22\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n23\t  existing_foundation: 1\n24\t  open_questions: 0\n25\t  external_dependency: 1\n26\t  human_process_gates: -1\n27\t  repo_access: 1\n28\t  architecture_claims: 1\n29\timplementation_type: null\n30\tinvestigation_signals: null\n31\tai_implementability: High\n32\tai_implementability_score: 6\n33\tjira_key: null\n34\tbranch: null\n35\t---\n36\t# Workspace-aware artifacts-only serving in the MLflow tracking server\n37\t\n38\t## Description\n39\t\n40\tMakes the MLflow tracking server's `--artifacts-only` mode composable with the existing `--enable-workspaces` flag, so the artifacts-only server validates artifact requests against workspace (tenant) boundaries. When both flags are set, the server requires an explicit `--workspace-store-uri` and initializes only the workspace provider needed to validate artifact requests; tracking stores and model registry stores stay uninitialized and non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled. No new server flag is introduced — the workspace-aware behavior emerges from combining existing flags (Staff Engineer / SME Input). The design is fail-closed: if the workspace store is unreachable while workspace-aware mode is active, artifact requests are denied rather than served without validation.\n41\t\n42\t## Scope\n43\t\n44\t- Flag composition: allow `--artifacts-only` to combine with `--enable-workspaces`. When both are set, require an explicit `--workspace-store-uri` (fail to start with a clear error if it is missing) and initialize only the workspace provider needed to validate artifact requests. Tracking and model registry stores stay uninitialized; non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled.\n45\t- Workspace validation in artifact request handling: for each artifact request, resolve the artifact path to its parent experiment (artifacts are stored under experiment-scoped paths, e.g. `{experiment_id}/{run_id}/artifacts/`), look up the experiment's workspace assignment via the workspace store, and delegate authorization to the existing auth plugin infrastructure. Requests failing workspace validation receive HTTP 403 with an error indicating insufficient workspace permissions; requests with invalid or missing workspace context are denied, not served.\n46\t- Fail-closed behavior: if the workspace store is unreachable while workspace-aware mode is active, deny artifact requests (HTTP 503 indicating workspace validation is unavailable) rather than serve without validation.\n47\t- Backwards compatibility: `--artifacts-only` without `--enable-workspaces` retains current behavior; single-tenant deployments and configurations without workspace flags are unaffected. No existing API contracts, CLI arguments, or configuration keys are removed or changed in meaning. The flag combination itself is the activation switch — omitting either flag preserves current behavior.\n48\t- Audit logging of workspace access denials in artifact serving through server logs (P2, bundled — see HLR Traceability).\n49\t- Performance: measure the per-request overhead of workspace resolution (workspace store lookup plus auth plugin validation); implement short-TTL caching of workspace membership decisions if the overhead exceeds the threshold agreed with PM/Engineering, consistent with caching patterns already used in the tracking server's auth path.\n50\t- Initial implementation scoped to the S3 artifact store (the standard RHOAI configuration), with limitations for non-standard artifact roots documented.\n51\t\n52\t## Acceptance Criteria\n53\t\n54\t- Given a multi-tenant deployment where the artifacts-only instance is started with `--artifacts-only --enable-workspaces --workspace-store-uri kubernetes://` and a user who is a member of workspace A, when the user requests an artifact belonging to an experiment in workspace A, then the artifact is served successfully, measured by HTTP 200 response with correct artifact content.\n55\t- Given a user who is a member of workspace A but not workspace B, when the user requests an artifact belonging to an experiment in workspace B, then the request is denied, measured by HTTP 403 response with an error message indicating insufficient workspace permissions.\n56\t- Given `--artifacts-only` set without `--enable-workspaces`, when any authenticated user requests an artifact, then the artifact is served without workspace validation (current behavior preserved), measured by HTTP 200 response regardless of workspace membership.\n57\t- Given the workspace-aware artifacts-only instance running and the workspace store (Kubernetes API) unreachable, when a user requests an artifact, then the request is denied (fail-closed), measured by HTTP 503 response indicating workspace validation is unavailable.\n58\t- The server fails to start with a clear error when `--artifacts-only` and `--enable-workspaces` are both set without an explicit `--workspace-store-uri`.\n59\t- In combined mode, non-artifact API endpoints remain disabled and tracking/model registry stores remain uninitialized.\n60\t- Workspace access denials in artifact serving are logged through server logs.\n61\t- The per-request workspace resolution overhead is measured and reported, and short-TTL caching of workspace membership decisions is implemented if the agreed threshold is exceeded.\n62\t- Workspace validation in artifact serving is exercised with negative/adversarial paths (invalid or missing workspace context, crafted paths) and denies them.\n63\t\n64\t## HLR Traceability\n65\t\n66\t- HLR-1 (P0): `--artifacts-only` composable with `--enable-workspaces` and `--workspace-store-uri` for workspace-validated artifact serving.\n67\t- HLR-2 (P0): artifact requests validated against workspace membership; non-members denied with HTTP 403.\n68\t- HLR-3 (P0): fail-closed behavior when the workspace store is unreachable.\n69\t- HLR-4 (P0): single-tenant deployments and configurations without `--enable-workspaces` retain current behavior.\n70\t- HLR-8 (P2, bundled): audit logging for workspace access denials through server logs — bundled because denial logging is emitted from the same workspace-validation denial path in the artifact request handler that HLR-2/HLR-3 build; a separate epic would instrument the same call sites twice, and the log lines have no independent user surface, so the work is not separately schedulable.\n71\t\n72\t## Dependencies and Assumptions\n73\t\n74\t- Builds on the auth plugin's workspace resolution capability functioning in the artifacts-only context (tracking stores uninitialized) — the plugin work must land first.\n75\t- Approach (path-to-experiment resolvability, plugin initialization decoupling) is confirmed by the workspace resolution feasibility findings before implementation starts; if those findings redirect the approach, this epic's scope is rewritten accordingly.\n76\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bc40f63e2747ee2e","agent_id":"a2a87ed65c879dd0a","session_id":"acc56831-ee8f-4f4b-89df-cab5630bc453","uuid":"adf990dc-b96c-4232-bc5a-c0bfd5b96fed","timestamp":"2026-10-26T18:39:11.327Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id"