{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b218e6cc9b898f7c","type":"tool_result","content":"16:**Detailed**: MLflow is deployed as a containerized tracking server on port 5000, serving the MLflow UI, REST API, and artifact proxy behind the `kubernetes-auth` authentication plugin from the external `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins` package (v1.5.0). The server runs Python 3.12 under gunicorn/uvicorn on a UBI9-minimal base image, with Kubernetes service account bearer tokens providing authentication and the `mlflow/server/auth` module enforcing RBAC-based authorization with roles, permissions, and per-resource access control. The `--enable-workspaces` flag activates multi-tenant workspace isolation backed by a Kubernetes workspace store (`kubernetes://`), where each workspace maps to a Kubernetes namespace. The component surface includes 6 architecture components—the container entrypoint, gunicorn and uvicorn ASGI/WSGI servers, the mlflow and mlflow-skinny Python services, and the mlflow-tracing SDK—exposing 48 HTTP endpoints for experiment tracking, model registry, gateway proxy, assistant, job execution, and MCP server APIs. External integrations include S3-compatible storage via boto3, Google Cloud Storage, and the Kubernetes API for project execution as Jobs. [source: Dockerfile.konflux:49-58, 76-80, requirements/konflux-pypi.in:13, mlflow/server/__init__.py:218-226, mlflow/server/auth/__init__.py:229-367, mlflow/tracking/request_auth/kubernetes_request_auth_provider.py:203-258, mlflow/projects/kubernetes.py:8-9, 32-49]\n20:MLflow operates in RHOAI as a multi-tenant experiment tracking and model registry server. The container entrypoint (`Dockerfile.konflux:80`) starts the MLflow tracking server with three key flags that define its RHOAI-specific behavior: `--app-name kubernetes-auth` loads the server-side authentication plugin from the separately packaged `mlflow-kubernetes-plugins` (v1.5.0, installed from PyPI via hermetic Konflux build), `--enable-workspaces` activates namespace-scoped workspace isolation, and `--workspace-store-uri kubernetes://` delegates workspace storage to the Kubernetes API. [source: Dockerfile.konflux:80, requirements/konflux-pypi.in:13]\n22:The authentication architecture has two layers. On the server side, the `--app-name kubernetes-auth` entry point loads an auth middleware plugin that intercepts API requests. On the client side, `mlflow/tracking/request_auth/kubernetes_request_auth_provider.py` provides two auth providers activated via `MLFLOW_TRACKING_AUTH`: `kubernetes` (bearer token only) and `kubernetes-namespaced` (bearer token plus `X-MLFLOW-WORKSPACE` header derived from the pod's service account namespace). Both resolve credentials from the Kubernetes service account token file at `/var/run/secrets/kubernetes.io/serviceaccount/token`, falling back to kubeconfig when running outside a cluster. The core `mlflow/server/auth/__init__.py` module provides RBAC enforcement with role-based permissions, per-resource access control for experiments, registered models, gateway endpoints, and workspaces. [source: mlflow/tracking/request_auth/kubernetes_request_auth_provider.py:1-11, 38-41, 203-294, mlflow/server/auth/__init__.py:229-367, mlflow/server/__init__.py:218-226]\n47:| Dockerfile.konflux:CMD | Container entrypoint | [\"python3.12\", \"-m\", \"mlflow\", \"server\", \"--host\", \"0.0.0.0\", \"--port\", \"5000\", \"--app-name\", \"kubernetes-auth\", \"--enable-workspaces\", \"--workspace-store-uri\", \"kubernetes://\", \"--serve-artifacts\"] |\n261:| Tracking Server API | All | kubernetes-auth plugin (Kubernetes service account bearer token) | Flask application via mlflow-kubernetes-plugins entry point | RBAC with role-based per-resource permissions |\n286:- **Entry and service surface:** Clients connect to the MLflow Tracking Server on port 5000. The `kubernetes-auth` plugin intercepts inbound requests and validates Kubernetes service account bearer tokens from the `Authorization` header. When workspaces are enabled, the `X-MLFLOW-WORKSPACE` header routes requests to the appropriate namespace-scoped workspace. The Flask application dispatches authenticated requests to tracking, model registry, artifact, gateway proxy, assistant, job, and MCP server API handlers. The `mlflow-skinny` service identity exposes the same surface with a minimal dependency set. [source: Dockerfile.konflux:76-80, mlflow/server/__init__.py:68-88, mlflow/tracking/request_auth/kubernetes_request_auth_provider.py:229-258]\n289:- **Security context:** The `kubernetes-auth` plugin and RBAC module enforce authentication and authorization across all tracking server endpoints. 19 environment-variable secrets reference API keys for external LLM providers (OpenAI, Anthropic, Cohere, Mistral, etc.) and cloud storage credentials. The `cryptography` package (>=43.0.0) provides TLS support via OpenSSL on the UBI9 base image. [source: mlflow/server/auth/__init__.py:229-367, mlflow/tracking/request_auth/kubernetes_request_auth_provider.py:38-41, pyproject.toml:37]\n---OPERATOR---\n14:**Short**: Kubernetes operator that manages the lifecycle of MLflow Tracking Server instances on OpenShift, including Helm-based deployment, Gateway API routing, namespace-scoped workspace RBAC, and integration with the RHOAI platform Auth service. [source: cmd/main.go:171, internal/controller/mlflow_controller.go:539, internal/controller/namespace_rbac_controller.go:67]\n16:**Detailed**: mlflow-operator is a controller-runtime operator that reconciles three custom resources — `MLflow` (mlflow.opendatahub.io/v1), `MLflowOperator` (components.platform.opendatahub.io/v1alpha1), and `MLflowConfig` (mlflow.kubeflow.org/v1) — to deploy and manage MLflow Tracking Server instances on RHOAI. The MLflow controller renders Helm charts into Kubernetes resources (Deployments, Services, PersistentVolumeClaims, CronJobs, NetworkPolicies) and conditionally creates Gateway API HTTPRoutes, OpenShift ConsoleLinks, and Prometheus ServiceMonitors based on cluster capability discovery. A dedicated NamespaceRBACReconciler watches the platform Auth CRD (`services.platform.opendatahub.io/v1alpha1`) and labeled namespaces to propagate view/edit RoleBindings for workspace-level access control. The operator uses leader election, caches filtered by label selectors, and field-selector-scoped watches for shared RBAC objects to minimize API server load. [source: cmd/main.go:171, 272, 300, internal/controller/mlflow_controller.go:473-552, internal/controller/namespace_rbac_controller.go:48-106, internal/controller/routing.go:46-179]\n20:mlflow-operator follows a multi-controller architecture built on controller-runtime v0.23.3. The primary **MLflowReconciler** owns the full lifecycle of MLflow Tracking Server deployments: it renders bundled Helm charts into Kubernetes objects (Deployments, Services, Secrets, ServiceAccounts, PersistentVolumeClaims, CronJobs, and NetworkPolicies) and applies them via server-side apply with owner references. The controller conditionally extends its watch surface at startup using the Kubernetes discovery API — adding Gateway API HTTPRoute, OpenShift ConsoleLink, and Prometheus ServiceMonitor watches only when those CRDs are present in the cluster. This discovery-gated pattern avoids hard dependencies on optional platform APIs. [source: internal/controller/mlflow_controller.go:528-552, internal/controller/routing.go:46-179]\n97:| helm.sh/helm/v3 | v3.19.2 | Yes |  | Go module dependency |\n148:| mlflow-operator-manager-role | gateway.networking.k8s.io | httproutes | create, delete, get, list, patch, update, watch |\n192:| manager-role | gateway.networking.k8s.io | httproutes | create, delete, get, list, patch, update, watch |\n275:- **Control plane reconciliation:** The operator watches MLflow, MLflowOperator, and MLflowConfig CRs, ConfigMaps, and owned resources (Deployments, Services, Jobs, PVCs, Secrets, ServiceAccounts). On reconciliation, the MLflowReconciler renders Helm charts into Kubernetes objects and applies them via the Kubernetes API (port 6443, TLS, ServiceAccount token auth). Owned resources are label-filtered (`app=mlflow`) to minimize watch bandwidth. [source: cmd/main.go:293-318, internal/controller/mlflow_controller.go:473-552]\n276:- **Conditional platform integration:** At startup, the operator probes the Kubernetes discovery API for Gateway API, ConsoleLink, ServiceMonitor, and MLflowOperator CRDs. When present, additional watches and owned-resource management are activated; when absent, the operator operates without those integrations. [source: internal/controller/routing.go:46-179, internal/controller/mlflow_controller.go:528-552]\n302:| Gateway API | HTTPRoute CRUD | runtime-transport |  | HTTPS | TLS 1.2+ | Manage Gateway API routing resources |\n320:| gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1/HTTPRoute | Controller watch (Owns) |  |  | Kubernetes API | TLS | MLflowReconciler |","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8cd41ae82e822a84","agent_id":"a77de1b6727c54ebd","session_id":"acc56831-ee8f-4f4b-89df-cab5630bc453","uuid":"c7a3f503-208c-44af-a1f6-9b134872befd","timestamp":"2026-10-26T18:42:24.971Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b218e6cc9b898f7c","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8009"}
